- Edge cases;
- Prohibited outcomes.

Omit irrelevant dimensions.

Do not replace specifics with vague quality claims.

## 7. Requirement Traceability

Assign stable requirement identifiers when useful.

For each material requirement, preserve traceability to:

- The feature outcome;
- Supporting evidence or decision;
- Likely affected project surface;
- Acceptance criteria;
- Verification evidence.

Do not invent exact file paths or symbols that were not established.

Label uncertain project surfaces as candidates and require the outside model to verify them before editing.

## 8. Acceptance Criteria

List discrete, observable acceptance criteria.

Each criterion must establish:

- Behavior to prove;
- Success condition;
- Relevant failure or negative conditions;
- Relevant boundary conditions;
- Relevant integration conditions;
- Relevant regression conditions.

Avoid criteria that prove only that:

- A file exists;
- A function was added;
- A test was written;
- A command exited successfully;
- A mock returned an expected value.

Those may be supporting evidence but are not sufficient when the feature requires user-visible or system-visible behavior.

## 9. Evidence Matrix

Map every acceptance criterion to:

- The requirement it proves;
- Likely implementation surface;
- Check that can prove or falsify it;
- Expected evidence;
- Whether the check is automated, manual, environment-dependent, or unavailable.

Require exact results.

Do not allow “looks good” or “tests passed” as complete evidence.

## 10. Implementation Workflow

Direct the outside model through these phases.

### A. Recontextualize

Require it to:

- Inspect applicable project instructions;
- Inspect architecture and design material;
- Inspect relevant implementation paths;
- Inspect callers and consumers;
- Inspect tests;
- Inspect schemas;
- Inspect configuration;
- Inspect generated-file rules;
- Inspect documentation;
- Inspect current workspace state;
- Verify the embedded feature contract against current project reality;
- Resolve discoverable implementation details from evidence;
- Identify contradictions with authoritative project rules;
- Produce a concise visible interpretation and living plan;
- Keep detailed private reasoning private.

Do not reopen settled product decisions merely because several implementation approaches are possible.

### B. Plan the Complete Change

Require it to identify:

- Owning components;
- Entry points;
- Upstream effects;
- Downstream effects;
- Data changes;
- Interface changes;
- State changes;
- Migration work;
- Compatibility work;
- Failure handling;
- Documentation changes;
- Generated artifacts;
- Tests;
- Validation;
- Rollout or rollback work;
- Adversarial review targets.

Require the plan to be updated when evidence changes the approach.

### C. Establish Proof

Require it to:

- Define the acceptance oracle before implementation;
- Reproduce current behavior when relevant;
- Establish a meaningful pre-change proof when practical;
- Identify how every acceptance criterion will be verified;
- Avoid ceremonial failing tests that do not prove the feature.

### D. Implement

Require it to:

- Implement the complete feature;
- Follow current project architecture where appropriate;
- Reuse existing capabilities when they genuinely fit;
- Avoid parallel systems;
- Avoid speculative abstraction;
- Avoid unrelated refactors;
- Preserve existing contracts unless intentionally changed;
- Handle realistic failures;
- Handle relevant edge cases;
- Add or update appropriate tests;
- Update affected documentation;
- Update affected schemas and configuration;
- Update required generated artifacts;
- Update operational controls when applicable;
- Preserve unrelated workspace changes;
- Verify every write or mutation.

Prohibit weakening, deleting, or bypassing valid tests merely to make the change pass.

### E. Verify

Require it to:

- Verify every acceptance criterion;
- Run applicable focused tests;
- Run applicable broader regression tests;
- Run applicable formatting, linting, type, build, schema, packaging, installation, or generated-file checks;
- Exercise a representative real path;
- Inspect actual output, state, UI behavior, files, logs, responses, or generated artifacts;
- Test relevant failures;
- Test relevant boundaries;
- Test relevant integration behavior;
- Test relevant regression behavior;
- Distinguish automated, manual, unavailable, skipped, environment-dependent, and pre-existing results;
- Record exact commands or actions and exact outcomes.

Passing newly written unit tests is not sufficient when the feature requires broader observable behavior.

### F. Adversarial Review and Repair

Require a separate fresh skeptical review after ordinary verification.

Try to falsify the implementation by checking:

- Whether it solves the intended outcome;
- Whether tests would fail if the feature were removed or bypassed;
- Whether callers or consumers were missed;
- Whether alternate entry points were missed;
- Whether generated artifacts were missed;
- Whether migration or compatibility work was missed;
- Whether permissions are correct;
- Whether failure behavior is correct;
- Whether retries can duplicate effects;
- Whether cancellation can leave inconsistent state;
- Whether concurrency can create races;
- Whether recovery is realistic;
- Whether error handling hides failures;
- Whether mocks bypass the behavior being proved;
- Whether documentation is stale;
- Whether configuration is incomplete;
- Whether the diff contains accidental scope;
- Whether secrets or local artifacts were introduced;
- Whether unsupported claims remain.

Turn credible findings into checks.

Repair all clear in-scope defects.

Rerun affected verification.

### G. Completion Gate

Do not declare completion until:

- The intended feature outcome is satisfied;
- Every applicable acceptance criterion has evidence;
- A representative real path works;
- Relevant failure and edge cases are covered;
- Relevant integration and regression behavior remains valid;
- Documentation, schemas, configuration, and generated surfaces are aligned;
- The adversarial review found no unresolved material defect;
- Remaining risks are explicit;
- Unverified behavior is labeled unverified.

## 11. Safety Boundaries

Prohibit:

- Invented project details;
- Unsupported architecture;
- Unrelated redesign;
- Destructive actions without authorization;
- Secret or credential exposure;
- Unnecessary sensitive-data duplication;
- Silent public-contract changes;
- Silent schema changes;
- False claims of successful testing;
- False claims of completed writes;
- Unauthorized commit, push, merge, publish, deploy, release, external notification, or other external completion action.

Require preservation of unrelated workspace changes.

When write access does not exist, require patch-ready files and exact integration instructions instead of pretending the project was changed.

## 12. Final Report

Require the outside model to report:

- Contextualized implemented outcome;
- Final scope;
- Key decisions;
- What changed;
- Why it changed;
- Files or project areas changed;
- Acceptance-criterion evidence;
- Exact verification actions and results;
- Adversarial findings;
- Repairs made;
- Documentation, schema, migration, rollout, rollback, or operational effects;
- Manual checks;
- Remaining risks;
- Genuine blockers;
- Actions not performed.

The final report must distinguish actual completed work from proposed, skipped, unavailable, or unverified work.

